# attack — success against shallow wildlife
#
# The third arm of the verb, and genuinely different from success.cli rather than a second seed of
# it: a chicken has no anatomy to wound and no fight to have, so the first blow kills outright and
# narration carries on. Everything BEFORE the kill is the same layer — the medium is gathered, an
# attack is drawn from it, and its modus mentis is what the blow teaches.
#
# It is deliberately not slay: that verb needs a tool, is difficulty 3, and is about butchery. This
# is a blow that happened to be enough.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Yard" --period dawn --observe-only "chicken"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

# There before the blow, so the absence asserted at the end is a removal and not a scene that never
# had one.
expect-state npcs chicken

strategy succeed
goal attack
advance 12 180

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180

expect SUCCESS
expect-verb attack

# The blow and its lesson — the half this shares with a fight-opening attack.
expect First blow:
expect Modus mentis
expect-outcome first_blow

# The half it does not: the bird is dead and there is no fight, so the phase hands back to narration
# rather than to the fight screen.
expect Slain:
expect-outcome npc_slayn
expect-no-outcome fight_trigger
# Gone from the scene entirely rather than standing there dead: RemoveNpcFromPlay drops it from
# scene.Npcs, so the assertion is an absence — the same one cli/outcome/npc_slayn/ makes.
expect-no-state npcs chicken
quit
